.\" tzim(1) — manual page. Kept in the repository, installed by the .deb, the
.\" .rpm, the Homebrew formula and the release archives. `tests/packaging.rs`
.\" fails when an option in `tzim --help` is missing here, so the page cannot
.\" drift into describing a different program.
.TH TZIM 1 "2026-10-25" "tzim 0.13.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tzim \- shrink a file without ever losing information
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tzim
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIPATH\fR...
.br
.B tzim
\fB\-\-info\fR | \fB\-\-list\-formats\fR | \fB\-\-check\-deps\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR | \fB\-\-version\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tzim
rewrites files into a smaller and equivalent form. It never performs a lossy or
irreversible transformation: no quality, resolution, bit depth or frame
reduction, no conversion to another format, and no metadata, comment or
attachment removal unless \fB\-\-strip\-metadata\fR asks for a fixed,
documented list to be removed. Every candidate is verified against a
per-format contract before it replaces anything, and a candidate that is not
strictly smaller is discarded, leaving the original byte for byte as it was.
With \fB\-\-strip\-metadata\fR an equal-size rewrite is accepted, because
zeroing a timestamp removes information without removing bytes.
.PP
Each path is an input object: a single file, a directory, or both mixed in one
run. A bare file path is complete on its own, so a single file never needs a
wrapper directory. Directories are read at the top level unless
.B \-\-recursive
is given. Symbolic links are skipped unless
.B \-\-follow\-symlinks
is given. Duplicate paths are processed once.
.PP
Handlers compiled into this build: PNG, APNG, gzip, the ZIP family, ICO/CUR,
baseline JPEG (opt-in, see
.BR \-\-formats ),
classic TIFF with strips, and zlib streams. Everything else is reported as
unsupported or skipped, with a reason.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Path selection
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-recursive
Descend into directories.
.TP
.B \-\-include \fIEXT\fR,... 
Only process files with these extensions.
.TP
.B \-\-exclude \fIEXT\fR,...
Skip files with these extensions. Exclusion wins over inclusion.
.TP
.B \-\-min\-size \fIBYTES\fR
Skip files smaller than this (default: 0).
.TP
.B \-\-max\-size \fIBYTES\fR
Skip files larger than this (default: 2147483648).
.TP
.B \-\-max\-pixels \fIN\fR
Skip images with more pixels than this (default: 100000000).
.TP
.B \-\-formats \fILIST\fR
Only process these formats: png, gzip, zip, ico, jpeg, tiff, zlib, fits. Naming
jpeg here is what enables the JPEG handler, which is off by default because it is
the riskiest handler and typically saves only 1\-3 %, and the FITS handler does
nothing at all without \fB\-\-fits\-tile\fR.
.SS Output
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Suppress the per-file lines. The summary is still printed.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write newline-delimited JSON on stdout, one object per file plus a summary
object. Never coloured.
.TP
.B \-\-color \fIWHEN\fR
Colour the report: \fIauto\fR (default), \fIalways\fR or \fInever\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Same as \fB\-\-color=never\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-progress
Never draw the progress line, even on a terminal.
.SS Work
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-dry\-run
Do all the work, write nothing, report what would change. The original is not
touched and every file is guaranteed byte-identical (G1).
.TP
.B \-j, \-\-jobs \fIN\fR
Maximum number of files processed at once (default: min(cores, 4), or
min(cores, 2) with \fB\-\-zopfli\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-fail\-fast
Stop after the first failed file. Other independent failures do not stop the
run without this flag.
.TP
.B \-\-zopfli
Use the slower and stronger DEFLATE encoder for PNG, gzip, ZIP, TIFF and zlib.
Typically 1\-5 % better than the default and 10\-100 times slower.
.TP
.B \-\-fits\-tile
FITS: rewrite an uncompressed image HDU as a tile\-compressed binary table, the
shape \fIfpack\fR writes. Off by default, because the file a reader sees is a
different file: an \fIIMAGE\fR HDU becomes a \fIBINTABLE\fR, and a program that
reads \fINAXIS1\fR of the first HDU reads a table width instead of an image row.
Quantisation is disabled, so the array is bit\-identical after the round trip,
and a rewrite that is not strictly smaller is discarded rather than written.
.TP
.B \-\-nested
Also optimize supported files inside archives.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-depth \fIN\fR
Nesting depth limit for \fB\-\-nested\fR (default: 3).
.TP
.B \-\-max\-uncompressed \fIBYTES\fR
Refuse archives that expand beyond this (default: 8589934592), which is the
decompression-bomb guard.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-ratio \fIN\fR
Refuse archives whose expansion ratio is above N:1 (default: 1000).
.TP
.B \-\-memory\-budget \fIBYTES\fR
Skip a file whose worst-case memory cost would exceed this, before it is read.
The default is derived from the other limits, so it never skips a file that
\fB\-\-max\-size\fR, \fB\-\-max\-pixels\fR and \fB\-\-max\-uncompressed\fR
admit; set it to what the machine has. The budget is per file, so
\fB\-\-jobs\fR files can be charged at once. A file above it is skipped, not
failed, and is left untouched.
.TP
.B \-\-strip\-metadata
Remove a fixed, documented list of embedded metadata: text chunks and
timestamps in PNG, name and comment fields in gzip, archive and entry comments
in ZIP, Exif and comments in JPEG. This is the one opt-in exception to the
no-removal rule; it is never on by default and never implied by another flag.
.TP
.B \-\-follow\-symlinks
Resolve and follow symbolic links.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-hardlinks
Process files with more than one hard link. By default they are skipped,
because replacing one link breaks the sharing without saying so.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-root\-ownership\-change
When running as root, replace files owned by another user.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-fsync
Skip fsync before the rename. Faster, less durable across a power loss.
.SS Information
.TP
.B \-i, \-\-info
Print what this program is: version, promise, formats, the verification level of
this build, links.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-formats
List the handlers compiled into this build and what each one preserves.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-deps
Print the external programs needed at run time. There are none.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print the full option list.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print the version on one line.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including files that were already optimal, skipped, unsupported or
refused by contract.
.TP
.B 1
At least one file failed. The other files were still processed.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage. Nothing was processed.
.TP
.B 3
Fatal error: the run could not be completed.
.TP
.B 4
Verification failure: a candidate did not satisfy its contract. The affected
file was not replaced. This is a bug in tzim, not in the input.
.PP
When several conditions hold at once the highest of 4, 3, 2 and 1 wins. A closed
stdout does not change the exit code the run earned.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Set and non-empty turns colour off (https://no-color.org/).
.TP
.B CLICOLOR_FORCE
Set to anything but \fI0\fR turns colour on, even through a pipe.
.TP
.B CLICOLOR
Set to \fI0\fR turns colour off.
.TP
.B TERM
\fIdumb\fR turns colour off.
.PP
Colour and the progress line need a terminal. Both are presentation only: the
same input produces the same bytes with or without them.
.SH FILES
None. There is no configuration file, no cache and no state. The program opens
no sockets and sends no telemetry.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Optimize one file:
.PP
.RS 4
tzim photo.png
.RE
.PP
Optimize a tree, reporting what would change without writing:
.PP
.RS 4
tzim ./photos \-r \-\-dry\-run
.RE
.PP
Squeeze a PNG as far as this tool can, accepting the time it takes:
.PP
.RS 4
tzim ./photos \-r \-\-zopfli \-\-formats png
.RE
.PP
Write one JSON object per file for a script:
.PP
.RS 4
tzim ./photos \-r \-\-json | jq \-r 'select(.type=="file") | .path'
.RE
.PP
Optimize files inside a ZIP archive as well:
.PP
.RS 4
tzim backup.zip \-\-nested
.RE
.SH LIMITATIONS
Rewriting a file replaces it, so its inode is new: permission bits and
timestamps are preserved, ACLs, extended attributes and hard links are not.
Files owned by another user are skipped unless tzim runs as root with
\fB\-\-allow\-root\-ownership\-change\fR. BigTIFF, tiled TIFF, LZW and CCITT
TIFF, progressive and arithmetic-coded JPEG, signed and ZIP64 archives, and
zlib streams with a preset dictionary are refused with a reason rather than
approximated. The full list is in \fIdocs/formats.md\fR and
\fIdocs/guarantees.md\fR.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gzip (1),
.BR zip (1),
.BR optipng (1),
.BR jpegtran (1)
.PP
Full documentation: https://github.com/RUdolf1517/tzim/tree/main/docs
